<body>

    <div class="textbook-container">

        <header class="textbook-header">
            <h1>Uniform Circular Motion</h1>
            <div class="subtitle">Chapter 4: Kinematics in Two Dimensions</div>
        </header>

        <!-- Introduction Card -->
        <section class="card">
            <div class="card-header">
                <div class="card-icon">1</div>
                <h2 class="card-title">Introduction</h2>
            </div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <p>
                    In our study of motion so far, we have primarily dealt with linear motion along a straight path. However, the universe is full of rotating objects—from electrons orbiting an atom to planets orbiting stars.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <strong>Uniform Circular Motion (UCM)</strong> occurs when an object moves in a circular path at a constant speed. While the speed is constant, the velocity is not. This distinction is the key to understanding the forces at play.
                </p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- Definition Card -->
        <section class="card">
            <div class="card-header">
                <div class="card-icon">D</div>
                <h2 class="card-title">Quick Definition</h2>
            </div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <p>
                    <em>Uniform Circular Motion</em> is the motion of an object traveling at a constant (uniform) speed on a circular path.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Even though the speed ($v$) remains constant, the direction of the velocity vector changes continuously. Because velocity is a vector quantity (magnitude + direction), a change in direction means the object is <strong>accelerating</strong>.
                </p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- Formula Card 1: Centripetal Acceleration -->
        <section class="card formula-card" id="formula-1">
            <div class="card-header">
                <div class="card-icon">F</div>
                <h2 class="card-title">Main Formula: Centripetal Acceleration</h2>
            </div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <p>Since the direction of velocity changes, there must be an acceleration directed toward the center of the circle. This is called <strong>centripetal acceleration</strong> ($a_c$).</p>

                <div class="formula-display">
                    $$ a_c = \frac{v^2}{r} $$
                </div>

                <div class="formula-actions">
                    <button class="btn btn-explain" onclick="toggleExplanation('explain-1', this)">
                        <span>ℹ️ Explain Formula</span>
                    </button>
                    <button class="btn btn-lab" onclick="toggleLab('lab-1', this)">
                        <span>🧪 Open Formula Lab</span>
                    </button>
                </div>

                <!-- Hidden Explanation Panel -->
                <div id="explain-1" class="interactive-panel">
                    <h4>Understanding the Variables</h4>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>$a_c$:</strong> Centripetal acceleration (meters per second squared, $m/s^2$).</li>
                        <li><strong>$v$:</strong> Tangential speed (meters per second, $m/s$). This is the constant speed of the object along the curve.</li>
                        <li><strong>$r$:</strong> Radius of the circular path (meters, $m$).</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p><strong>Key Insight:</strong> The acceleration is inversely proportional to the radius ($r$). This means for a tight turn (small $r$), the acceleration required to stay on the path is much larger than for a gentle turn (large $r$).</p>
                </div>

                <!-- Hidden Lab Panel -->
                <div id="lab-1" class="interactive-panel">
                    <h4>Formula Lab: Calculate $a_c$</h4>
                    <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; margin-bottom: 1rem;">Adjust the values to see how centripetal acceleration changes.</p>
                    <div class="lab-controls">
                        <div class="control-group">
                            <label>Velocity ($v$) [m/s]</label>
                            <input type="range" min="1" max="50" value="10" id="input-v" oninput="calculateAC()">
                            <span id="val-v">10</span> m/s
                        </div>
                        <div class="control-group">
                            <label>Radius ($r$) [m]</label>
                            <input type="range" min="1" max="100" value="20" id="input-r" oninput="calculateAC()">
                            <span id="val-r">20</span> m
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="lab-result">
                        $a_c = $ <span id="result-ac">5.00</span> $m/s^2$
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- Key Concepts Card -->
        <section class="card">
            <div class="card-header">
                <div class="card-icon">K</div>
                <h2 class="card-title">Key Concepts</h2>
            </div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Period ($T$):</strong> The time it takes to complete one full revolution. Measured in seconds.</li>
                    <li><strong>Frequency ($f$):</strong> The number of revolutions per unit time (usually Hertz, Hz). $f = \frac{1}{T}$.</li>
                    <li><strong>Tangential Velocity:</strong> The velocity vector at any point is tangent to the circle. Its magnitude is constant, but direction changes.</li>
                    <li><strong>Centripetal Force:</strong> The net force causing the centripetal acceleration. It is not a new "type" of force, but the role played by tension, gravity, or friction.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- Formula Card 2: Centripetal Force -->
        <section class="card formula-card" id="formula-2">
            <div class="card-header">
                <div class="card-icon">F</div>
                <h2 class="card-title">Main Formula: Newton's Second Law in Rotation</h2>
            </div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <p>According to Newton's Second Law ($F=ma$), if there is a centripetal acceleration, there must be a centripetal force ($F_c$).</p>

                <div class="formula-display">
                    $$ F_c = \frac{mv^2}{r} $$
                </div>

                <div class="formula-actions">
                    <button class="btn btn-explain" onclick="toggleExplanation('explain-2', this)">
                        <span>ℹ️ Explain Formula</span>
                    </button>
                </div>

                <!-- Hidden Explanation Panel -->
                <div id="explain-2" class="interactive-panel">
                    <h4>Understanding the Variables</h4>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>$F_c$:</strong> Centripetal force (Newtons, $N$).</li>
                        <li><strong>$m$:</strong> Mass of the object (kilograms, $kg$).</li>
                        <li><strong>$v$:</strong> Tangential speed ($m/s$).</li>
                        <li><strong>$r$:</strong> Radius ($m$).</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p><strong>Direction:</strong> The force is always directed inward, toward the center of the circle. Without this inward force, the object would continue in a straight line (Newton's First Law).</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- Characteristics Card -->
        <section class="card">
            <div class="card-header">
                <div class="card-icon">C</div>
                <h2 class="card-title">Characteristics of UCM</h2>
            </div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <p>To fully understand UCM, we must distinguish between what changes and what stays the same:</p>
                <div class="table-responsive">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Quantity</th>
                                <th>Behavior in UCM</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Speed ($|v|$)</td>
                                <td>Constant</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Velocity ($\vec{v}$)</td>
                                <td>Changes (Direction)</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Acceleration ($\vec{a}$)</td>
                                <td>Constant magnitude, directed toward center</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Net Force ($\Sigma \vec{F}$)</td>
                                <td>Constant magnitude, directed toward center</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Kinetic Energy ($KE$)</td>
                                <td>Constant ($\frac{1}{2}mv^2$)</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- Applications Card -->
        <section class="card">
            <div class="card-header">
                <div class="card-icon">A</div>
                <h2 class="card-title">Real-World Applications</h2>
            </div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Satellites:</strong> Earth's gravity provides the centripetal force keeping satellites in orbit.</li>
                    <li><strong>Banked Curves:</strong> Roads are tilted on curves so that the normal force from the road helps provide the centripetal force, reducing reliance on friction.</li>
                    <li><strong>Centrifuges:</strong> Machines that spin samples at high speeds to separate components of different densities based on the required centripetal force.</li>
                    <li><strong>Gravitron Rides:</strong> Amusement park rides where the walls push inward on riders to keep them pinned in place as the floor drops.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- Important Notes Card -->
        <section class="card" style="border-left: 5px solid #f59e0b;">
            <div class="card-header">
                <div class="card-icon" style="color: #f59e0b; background-color: #fffbeb;">!</div>
                <h2 class="card-title">Important Notes</h2>
            </div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <p><strong>Centrifugal vs. Centripetal:</strong></p>
                <p>
                    Students often confuse <strong>Centripetal Force</strong> (real, inward force) with <strong>Centrifugal Force</strong> (fictitious, outward force). 
                </p>
                <p>
                    "Centrifugal force" is simply the sensation of inertia you feel when your body wants to continue in a straight line while the car turns underneath you. In an inertial frame of reference, <strong>centrifugal force does not exist</strong>.
                </p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- Summary Card -->
        <section class="card">
            <div class="card-header">
                <div class="card-icon">S</div>
                <h2 class="card-title">Summary</h2>
            </div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <p>Uniform Circular Motion describes an object moving at a constant speed along a circular path. Because the direction of the velocity vector is constantly changing, the object experiences an acceleration directed toward the center of the circle, known as centripetal acceleration ($a_c = v^2/r$). This requires a corresponding net force directed inward, the centripetal force ($F_c = mv^2/r$).</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- Suggested Questions Card -->
        <section class="card">
            <div class="card-header">
                <div class="card-icon">Q</div>
                <h2 class="card-title">Suggested Questions</h2>
            </div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <ol>
                    <li>What happens to the centripetal acceleration if the speed of the object is doubled?</li>
                    <li>Why does the centripetal force do no work on the object?</li>
                    <li>Draw a free-body diagram for a car making a right turn on a flat road. Which force provides the centripetal force?</li>
                </ol>
            </div>
        </section>

        <footer class="textbook-footer">
            &copy; 2023 Interactive Physics Textbook. Generated by AI Tutor.
        </footer>
    </div>

    <!-- KaTeX JS -->
    <script defer src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/katex@0.16.8/dist/katex.min.js"></script>
    <script defer src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/katex@0.16.8/dist/contrib/auto-render.min.js"
        onload="renderMathInElement(document.body);"></script>

    <!-- Interactive Logic -->
    <script>
        // Toggle Explanation Panels
        function toggleExplanation(id, btn) {
            const panel = document.getElementById(id);
            const isActive = panel.classList.contains('active');

            // Close all other panels first (optional, keeps it clean)
            document.querySelectorAll('.interactive-panel').forEach(p => p.classList.remove('active'));

            if (!isActive) {
                panel.classList.add('active');
                btn.innerHTML = '<span>🔼 Hide Explanation</span>';
            } else {
                btn.innerHTML = '<span>ℹ️ Explain Formula</span>';
            }
        }

        // Toggle Lab Panels
        function toggleLab(id, btn) {
            const panel = document.getElementById(id);
            const isActive = panel.classList.contains('active');

            if (!isActive) {
                panel.classList.add('active');
                btn.innerHTML = '<span>🔼 Close Formula Lab</span>';
            } else {
                panel.classList.remove('active');
                btn.innerHTML = '<span>🧪 Open Formula Lab</span>';
            }
        }

        // Formula Lab Logic for a_c = v^2 / r
        function calculateAC() {
            const v = parseFloat(document.getElementById('input-v').value);
            const r = parseFloat(document.getElementById('input-r').value);

            // Update labels
            document.getElementById('val-v').textContent = v;
            document.getElementById('val-r').textContent = r;

            // Calculate
            const ac = (Math.pow(v, 2) / r).toFixed(2);

            // Update Result
            document.getElementById('result-ac').textContent = ac;
        }
    </script>
</body>
